  <h1>Rapid Test vs PCR</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Rapid antigen tests give a result in minutes at the point of care but are less sensitive; PCR detects nucleic acid with higher sensitivity in a laboratory. Use rapid tests to screen fast and PCR to confirm.</p>

  <h2>Comparison table</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Rapid test (lateral flow)</th><th>PCR</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Target</th><td>Antigen / antibody</td><td>Nucleic acid (RNA/DNA)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Time to result</th><td>10–30 min</td><td>Hours</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Sensitivity</th><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Setting</th><td>Point of care</td><td>Laboratory</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Instrument</th><td>None (visual read)</td><td>Thermal cycler</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cost</th><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What each method detects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid test:</strong> a target protein (antigen) or the immune response (antibody) — visible line readout.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR:</strong> amplifies and detects pathogen genetic material — the reference standard for many infections.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Strengths</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid:</strong> fast, portable, instrument-free, low cost, easy to deploy in field and clinic.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR:</strong> highest sensitivity/specificity, detects low pathogen load, quantifiable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid:</strong> lower sensitivity, window period, subjective visual reading.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR:</strong> lab infrastructure, trained staff, longer turnaround, higher cost.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>False negative / positive considerations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid false negative:</strong> low viral/antigen load, testing outside the window.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR false negative:</strong> sample degradation or collection error; false positives from contamination are rare but possible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Confirmatory testing</h2>
  <p>Rapid tests are screening-grade; PCR is typically the confirmatory method when accuracy is critical.</p>

  <h2>Procurement considerations</h2>
  <p>Distributors often supply rapid tests for screening volume and partner with labs for PCR confirmation.</p>

  <h2>Related pages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/comparisons/rapid-test-vs-elisa/">Rapid Test vs ELISA</a></li>
    <li><a href="/comparisons/ns1-vs-pcr/">Dengue NS1 vs PCR</a></li>
  </ul>

  
  <h2>Which test to choose</h2>
  <p>Choose a <strong>rapid antigen test</strong> for fast, low-cost screening at the point of care; choose <strong>PCR</strong> when accuracy and confirmation are critical.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Recommended</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Point-of-care screening</td><td><strong>Rapid test</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Confirmation</td><td><strong>PCR</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Field / outbreak response</td><td><strong>Rapid test</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Low viral load</td><td><strong>PCR</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
